From c1d086f186358b968cd7c7a822dcf6008deef06d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karl Mehltretter <kmehltretter@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 07:20:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] module: reject out-of-range relocation target indices
To: Luis Chamberlain <mcgrof@kernel.org>,
    Petr Pavlu <petr.pavlu@suse.com>,
    Daniel Gomez <da.gomez@kernel.org>,
    Sami Tolvanen <samitolvanen@google.com>
Cc: Aaron Tomlin <atomlin@atomlin.com>,
    Ard Biesheuvel <ardb@kernel.org>,
    Nicolas Pitre <nico@fluxnic.net>,
    Russell King <linux@armlinux.org.uk>,
    "James E.J. Bottomley" <James.Bottomley@HansenPartnership.com>,
    Helge Deller <deller@gmx.de>,
    linux-modules@vger.kernel.org,
    linux-arm-kernel@lists.infradead.org,
    linux-parisc@vger.kernel.org,
    linux-riscv@lists.infradead.org,
    loongarch@lists.linux.dev,
    linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

The module loader validates section contents and names before calling
module_frob_arch_sections(), but does not check the sh_info target index
of SHT_REL and SHT_RELA sections. Several architecture hooks use sh_info
as an array index before apply_relocations() skips out-of-range indices.

On ARM with ARM_MODULE_PLTS, an out-of-range index makes the hook read a
section header outside the section table. On PA-RISC, a relocation which
requires a stub makes the hook read and then update an e_shnum-sized
heap array at that index. The tested malformed modules fault in these
hooks instead of being rejected.

Reject relocation sections whose target index is outside the section
table in the early ELF validity pass.

Fixes: c298be74492b ("parisc: fix module loading failure of large kernel modules")
Fixes: 7d485f647c1f ("ARM: 8220/1: allow modules outside of bl range")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Karl Mehltretter <kmehltretter@gmail.com>
---

Notes (review):
    Base: Linux 28924df2a08f ("Merge tag
    'perf-tools-fixes-for-v7.3-2026-09-07' of
    git://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/perf/perf-tools").
    
    At Linux b9b3e33b70b7 ("Merge tag 'trace-v7.2-rc6' of
    git://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/trace/linux-trace"), a
    custom Fragma harness used upstream Frama-C 33.0 (Arsenic) Eva to
    analyze a source-identical ARM module_frob_arch_sections() slice.
    Eva reported the out-of-range section-table pointer and sh_flags
    access.
    
    ARM32 A/B testing used QEMU 10.2.1 virt/TCG with a Cortex-A15 CPU,
    GCC 15.2.0, and multi_v7_defconfig plus VMSPLIT_2G. The control
    module loaded and unloaded before and after the change. Before the
    change, a module whose SHT_REL sh_info was changed from 10 to
    0x10000000 faulted at module_frob_arch_sections()+0x160. After the
    change, the same module was rejected with ENOEXEC and the
    invalid-index diagnostic.
    
    PA-RISC A/B testing used QEMU 10.2.1 B160L/TCG, hppa-linux-gcc
    8.1.0, binutils 2.30, and generic-32bit_defconfig. The control
    module loaded and unloaded before and after the change. The mutation
    changed only the four-byte sh_info field of
    .rela.text.unlikely.fragma_sh_info_init at file offset 0xae4 from
    big-endian 00 00 00 03 to 10 00 00 00. The section contains an
    R_PARISC_PCREL17F relocation counted by count_stubs().
    
    Before the change, the malformed module caused a Data TLB miss at
    module_frob_arch_sections()+0x11c. Disassembly maps that address to
    the stub_entries read used by WARN_ON(). The later store at +0x12c
    was not reached. After the change, the same module returned ENOEXEC,
    the kernel printed target index 268435456, and the guest reached its
    completion marker. Both PA-RISC kernels used byte-identical
    configurations.
    
    The patched tree also completed full vmlinux builds for ARM64
    defconfig with GCC 15.2.0, RISC-V defconfig plus RELOCATABLE with
    GCC 15.2.0, and LoongArch loongson64_defconfig with LLVM 21.1.8.
    MODULE_SECTIONS was enabled in the RISC-V build. These were
    build-only checks. A source audit found the same early sh_info
    indexing pattern in those three hooks. ARM32 and PA-RISC are the
    runtime-tested paths.

 kernel/module/main.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/module/main.c b/kernel/module/main.c
index d0e1e0bd2ad0..30c7a05488bc 100644
--- a/kernel/module/main.c
+++ b/kernel/module/main.c
@@ -1933,6 +1933,7 @@ static int elf_validity_ehdr(const struct load_info *info)
  * * Section array fits in the user provided data
  * * Section index 0 is NULL
  * * Section contents are inbounds
+ * * Relocation section target indices are inbounds
  *
  * Then updates @info with a &load_info->sechdrs pointer if valid.
  *
@@ -1983,6 +1984,12 @@ static int elf_validity_cache_sechdrs(struct load_info *info)
 	/* Validate contents are inbounds */
 	for (i = 1; i < info->hdr->e_shnum; i++) {
 		shdr = &sechdrs[i];
+		if ((shdr->sh_type == SHT_REL || shdr->sh_type == SHT_RELA) &&
+		    shdr->sh_info >= info->hdr->e_shnum) {
+			pr_err("Invalid ELF relocation section target index %u\n",
+			       shdr->sh_info);
+			return -ENOEXEC;
+		}
 		switch (shdr->sh_type) {
 		case SHT_NULL:
 		case SHT_NOBITS:

